recting the background shutdown command's output - bounded, low-risk, and an expected byproduct of the restart step rather than an unnecessary mutation. No failed services, no unsafe new network exposure, and package health remained consistent (manager=dnf) before and after.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fdb9a53dfbae4319\",\"cmd-4659103a775a41b7\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor created a self-signed CA with the exact required CN, installed it into the system trust store via update-ca-trust, and verified trust both immediately and after a full node restart with a directly observed boot-identity change. The mandatory restart protocol is fully and coherently evidenced (healthy baseline, acknowledgement, disruption, new boot id, recovered CA/trust state), and no unnecessary or unexplained mutations were introduced.\",\"limitations\":[\"Prepare phase was disabled/aliased to before_prepare, so no independent prepare-stage baseline exists beyond the initial snapshot; this does not affect assessment since the task required no preparation.\",\"Verification relied on the executor's own trust-store tooling (trust list, openssl verify) rather than an independent client validating a certificate issued by the new CA, though this is a reasonable and standard way to confirm system CA trust.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ca-created","requirement":"Create a local certificate authority named 'OS Comparison Root CA' on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"openssl req -x509 generated a self-signed CA certificate with CN=OS Comparison Root CA, CA:true basicConstraints, 10-year validity; subject/issuer/dates confirmed by output.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fdb9a53dfbae4319"]},{"id":"ca-trusted","requirement":"Install the CA certificate as a trusted system certificate authority","sta